@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. Implement JSON Patch, as specified in
+RFC 6902, with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`. It builds on
+JSON Pointer (RFC 6901), implemented as `resolvePointer` in `src/index.ts`.
+
+Export a function `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a JSON Patch (an array of
+operations) to a JSON document and returns the resulting document as a new value: it must not mutate its
+input. It throws an error when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. The patch must be an array of operation objects; each operation needs a string `op` that is one of
+   the six names, and a string `path` that is a valid JSON Pointer. Otherwise the patch is invalid and
+   `applyPatch` throws.
+2. `add`: with a `value`. On an object, sets the member (replacing it if present). On an array, inserts
+   at the index, where `-` appends and an index greater than the length is an error. Adding at the
+   empty path replaces the whole document.
+3. `remove`: deletes the member or array element at `path`. The target must exist; `-` is not a valid
+   target. The empty path is an error.
+4. `replace`: with a `value`. The target must exist; it is replaced. The empty path replaces the whole
+   document.
+5. `move`: with a `from` pointer. Removes the value at `from` and adds it at `path`. `from` must exist,
+   and a location cannot be moved into one of its own children (an error).
+6. `copy`: with a `from` pointer. Adds a copy of the value at `from` to `path`. `from` must exist.
+7. `test`: with a `value`. Succeeds when the value at `path` is deeply equal to `value`; otherwise the
+   patch fails and `applyPatch` throws.
+8. Operations apply in order; each sees the result of the previous ones. If one fails, `applyPatch`
+   throws and the input document is unchanged.
+9. The input document and patch are never mutated; the returned document shares no mutable structure
+   with the input that a later change to either could alter.
+10. Input is typed explicitly; the implementation has no runtime dependency.
+
+## Test classification
+
+- Happy path for each of the six operations: new behaviour, so these tests are red-first.
+- Edge and error paths: each criterion 1 to 9 has at least one test.
